  <h1 id="top">Vegan Jerky: The Complete Guide (What It Is, Brands & a Recipe)</h1>

  <div class="answer-box">
    <p><strong>Vegan jerky</strong> is a plant-based chewy snack designed to mimic beef jerky, made from ingredients like soy protein, seitan, mushrooms, pea protein, or jackfruit. It delivers similar salty, savory, umami-rich flavor and comparable protein per serving, and comes as both store-bought brands (Louisville Vegan Jerky, Pan's, Primal Strips) and homemade recipes made with soy curls.</p>
  </div>

  <ContentImage src="/images/vegan-jerky-hero.webp" alt="A pile of dark reddish-brown vegan jerky strips on a wooden board next to open packages of Louisville and Pan's mushroom jerky" eager={true} />

  <p>I make beef jerky for a living. So when my niece went vegan last summer and asked if I could send her a care package that wasn't just a jar of cured nothing, I had to sit with the question for a minute. <em>[Switches to serious face]</em> Turns out plant-based jerky has come a genuinely long way, and the good brands are legitimately delicious in their own right — not "for a vegan product," just delicious. This is what I learned tearing through a dozen brands and building a homemade recipe with Butler soy curls. Full honest guide: what it's actually made of, how it stacks up to beef, which brands are worth your $8, and a recipe you can bang out in the oven this afternoon.</p>

