  <h2 id="what-is-vegan-jerky">What Is Vegan Jerky</h2>
  <p><strong>Vegan jerky</strong> is a chewy, salty, savory snack designed to fill the same role as beef jerky using entirely plant-based ingredients. The base can be soy protein, wheat gluten (seitan), whole mushrooms, pea protein, or even jackfruit — each one gets marinated in soy sauce, liquid smoke, and spices, then dehydrated or slow-baked until it develops that classic tear-and-chew texture.</p>
  <p>The category has quietly matured. Ten years ago vegan jerky was mostly one company (Primal Strips) and a handful of small producers, and the texture was aggressively rubbery. Today there are dozens of legitimate brands, several with venture-scale backing (Pan's Mushroom Jerky raised money on Shark Tank in 2020), and the flavor and mouthfeel are close enough to the real thing that even lifelong meat eaters can enjoy a bag without feeling like they're eating a compromise.</p>

  <h2 id="what-vegan-jerky-is-made-of">What Vegan Jerky Is Made Of</h2>
  <p>Every vegan jerky brand starts from one of five base ingredients. This is the single most important thing to know about the category — the base determines the texture, the flavor ceiling, and who the product works for. Here is the honest breakdown of <strong>what vegan jerky is made of</strong>:</p>
  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Base ingredient</th><th>Texture</th><th>Brands that use it</th><th>Best for</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Soy protein / TVP / soy curls</strong></td><td>Chewy, closest to real jerky pull</td><td>Louisville Vegan Jerky, Unisoy, All Vegetarian, Perky Jerky, BeLeaf</td><td>Anyone chasing the closest beef-jerky mouthfeel</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Seitan (wheat gluten)</strong></td><td>Very meaty, dense, requires real chew</td><td>Homemade seitan jerky; some UNLIMEAT products</td><td>Serious chew-lovers with no gluten issues</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Whole mushrooms</strong></td><td>Delicate, breaks apart, umami-forward</td><td>Pan's, Country Archer Plant-Based, Eat the Change, Savory Wild</td><td>Whole-food eaters, gluten-free diners</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Pea protein / fava protein</strong></td><td>Firmer than soy, slightly more crumbly</td><td>Off-Piste Provisions, AGRO Power Jerky</td><td>People avoiding both soy and gluten</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Jackfruit</strong></td><td>Fibrous, stringy, pulled-pork-like</td><td>Jack & Friends</td><td>Top-nine allergen sufferers</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>If you've never had vegan jerky before, start with a soy-based option like <strong>Louisville Vegan Jerky</strong> — it's the closest to what your palate expects from the word "jerky." Then work outward: mushroom for a completely different (but genuinely great) snack, pea protein if you're avoiding soy, jackfruit if you're avoiding everything.</p>

  <ContentImage src="/images/vegan-jerky-ingredients.webp" alt="Soy curls, dried portabella mushrooms, and vital wheat gluten laid out on a wooden counter as the three main vegan jerky base ingredients" />

  <h2 id="is-beef-jerky-vegan">Is Beef Jerky Vegan</h2>
  <p>Short answer: <strong>no — beef jerky is not vegan</strong>. It is dried, seasoned beef. Beef is a cow. Cows are animals. It is also not vegetarian (which excludes meat) or pescatarian (which allows fish but not land meat). This sounds obvious written out, but the question shows up in search often enough that it deserves a straight answer instead of an assumption.</p>
  <p>Where confusion creeps in is that <strong>some jerky brands sell both</strong>. Perky Jerky, Country Archer, and Krave all have plant-based product lines that live right next to their beef lines on store shelves, in packaging that can look nearly identical. If you're vegan or shopping for a vegan friend, read the front of the bag — legitimate vegan jerky always says "vegan" or "plant-based" clearly on the front, and often carries the green <a href="https://vegan.org/certification/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Certified Vegan logo</a> (Vegan Action's third-party certification) somewhere on the label.</p>
  <p>Also worth knowing: some meat jerky uses <strong>Worcestershire sauce</strong> (which contains anchovies) or <strong>honey</strong> (a bee product), so even a jerky labeled "chicken-free" or "beef-free" isn't automatically vegan unless it says so. Beyond Meat, by the way, does not currently make jerky — when people ask "is Beyond Meat jerky vegan," they're usually thinking of Louisville Vegan Jerky or Pan's.</p>

  <h2 id="how-to-make-vegan-jerky">How to Make Vegan Jerky</h2>
  <p>The best entry point to <strong>how to make vegan jerky</strong> at home is Butler soy curls — they're the go-to home cook ingredient, they cost around $6 a bag, and they take on marinade beautifully once you squeeze the rehydration water out. This is the recipe I built for my niece and have run maybe fifteen times since. It works in a low oven; if you have a dehydrator (see our <a href="/best-dehydrator-for-jerky/">best dehydrator for jerky</a> guide), run it at 160°F for 4 to 6 hours instead.</p>

