  <ContentImage src="/images/vegan-jerky-recipe.webp" alt="Homemade vegan jerky made from marinated soy curls arranged on a baking sheet ready to go into the oven" />

  <p>A few things that separate a great homemade batch from a sad one:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Squeeze the water out harder than feels reasonable.</strong> A nut milk bag or clean tea towel is the move. Wet soy curls repel marinade; dry soy curls inhale it.</li>
    <li><strong>Rest the finished jerky overnight in a sealed container.</strong> The moisture redistributes and the texture goes from "pretty good" to "wait, I made this?"</li>
    <li><strong>Use regular soy sauce, not low-sodium.</strong> The salt is doing structural work here — it's cure and flavor at the same time. This is not the recipe to health-hack.</li>
    <li><strong>Liquid smoke is not optional if you want that campfire-jerky character.</strong> Half a tablespoon is enough; a full one gets aggressive.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For more variations — seitan jerky, mushroom jerky, teriyaki soy-curl — our upcoming vegan jerky recipes collection will collect them in one place. The base marinade above holds up across every plant protein I've tried it on.</p>

  <h2 id="best-vegan-jerky-brands">Best Vegan Jerky Brands</h2>
  <p>Every "best vegan jerky" article on the internet ranks brands with star scores I do not trust, because taste is personal and star ratings are theater. So here is the honest comparison of the brands that keep showing up in reviews (and on Whole Foods shelves), organized by what makes each one distinctive:</p>
  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Brand</th><th>Base</th><th>What it's known for</th><th>Best for</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
