      <tr><td><strong>Primal Strips (Primal Spirit Foods)</strong></td><td>Seitan or shiitake</td><td>The original wide-distribution vegan jerky; multiple bases; sold in health-food stores for 15+ years</td><td>Longtime vegan shoppers, seitan fans</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Pan's Mushroom Jerky</strong></td><td>Shiitake mushrooms</td><td>Shark Tank alum; entirely different snack from soy-based; umami-forward; flavors include zesty Thai, applewood BBQ, teriyaki</td><td>Whole-food eaters, gluten-free, mushroom lovers</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>All Vegetarian</strong></td><td>Non-GMO textured soy</td><td>The favorite in Make It Dairy Free's blind taste test; closest to childhood beef jerky texture</td><td>Value shoppers; texture purists</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Perky Jerky Vegan</strong></td><td>Soy powder</td><td>Wide grocery distribution (Whole Foods, some Krogers); milder flavor</td><td>Anyone who wants to buy vegan jerky in a regular grocery store</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Country Archer Plant-Based</strong></td><td>King oyster mushrooms</td><td>Best of the mushroom-based crowd per Make It Dairy Free; fruit-leather texture; coconut-aminos flavor base</td><td>Gluten-free + mushroom-tolerant shoppers</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Unisoy</strong></td><td>Soy protein</td><td>Solid all-rounder; wide flavor variety including pineapple habanero</td><td>Flavor adventurers</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Noble Jerky</strong></td><td>Soy (no wheat gluten)</td><td>Softer chew, unusual sour-and-peppercorn flavors, inconsistent piece size</td><td>Snackers who like softer textures</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Which one is <em>the</em> best? Honestly, that depends on which base you want and what flavor you like. LVJCO is the most-cited pick for people crossing over from beef jerky. Pan's is the most-cited pick for people who want a genuinely different snack. Both are worth the $6-8 to try. I am not going to fabricate star ratings — buy one bag of each of the top two, run your own taste test, and trust your own tongue over anyone else's list.</p>

  <ContentImage src="/images/vegan-jerky-brands.webp" alt="A lineup of Louisville Vegan Jerky, Primal Strips, Pan's Mushroom Jerky, and Country Archer plant-based bags on a wooden counter for comparison" />

  <h2 id="is-vegan-jerky-healthy">Is Vegan Jerky Healthy</h2>
  <p><strong>Is vegan jerky healthy?</strong> It's roughly a wash with beef jerky on most macros — comparable protein, comparable sodium, generally less fat, and zero cholesterol. Here is a typical head-to-head using per-1-oz-serving averages (numbers approximate; brand-to-brand variation is real):</p>
  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Per 1 oz (28 g) serving</th><th>Vegan jerky (soy-based avg)</th><th>Beef jerky (avg)</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Calories</td><td>70–100</td><td>80–120</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Protein</td><td>10–14 g</td><td>9–12 g</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fat</td><td>1–3 g</td><td>2–5 g</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Saturated fat</td><td>0 g</td><td>1–2 g</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cholesterol</td><td>0 mg</td><td>25–40 mg</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Sodium</td><td>300–600 mg</td><td>400–700 mg</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Sugar (added)</td><td>3–8 g</td><td>2–6 g</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>The honest read: <strong>protein is roughly equal</strong>, vegan wins on cholesterol and saturated fat, beef edges out on the "less added sugar" line, and both are aggressively salty because both are cured with salt. If you're watching sodium for cardiovascular reasons, neither is a great daily habit. If you're eating for protein, both work. Mushroom-based vegan jerky tends to be the lowest-calorie option in the category but also lowest in protein (typically 5-7 g per serving). Verify specific numbers via the <a href="https://fdc.nal.usda.gov/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">USDA FoodData Central</a> for the specific brand or ingredient you're eating.</p>

  <h2 id="louisville-vegan-jerky-company">Louisville Vegan Jerky Company</h2>
  <p><strong>Louisville Vegan Jerky Co.</strong> (usually shortened to LVJCO) is the most-cited vegan jerky brand on the internet and the current top-ranked result for the head keyword. Based in Louisville, Kentucky, the company was founded in 2012 by Stanley Chase III and has grown from a farmers-market operation into a wide-distribution brand carried by Whole Foods, Sprouts, and hundreds of independent grocers.</p>
  <p>What they make: textured soy protein jerky in a lineup that includes Smoked Black Pepper, Perfect Pepperoni, Chipotle Adobo, Bourbon Smoked Spice, and Carolina BBQ. Their soy comes from whole beans (not the isolate powder that gives some competitors a chalky finish), which is why the texture reads closer to real jerky than most alternatives.</p>
  <p>Where to buy: their own site at <a href="https://louisvillejerky.com/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">louisvillejerky.com</a>, Whole Foods (usually in the meat-snack aisle rather than the vegan section — mildly funny), and Amazon. Bags run around $7-8 for 3 oz. Not cheap per ounce, but the flavor lineup and the texture make it a legitimate contender for the "one bag to try" spot if you're vegan-curious.</p>

  <h2 id="soy-free-and-gluten-free">Soy-Free and Gluten-Free Vegan Jerky</h2>
  <p>If you're vegan and also avoiding soy or gluten, most of the category is closed to you — but the good news is that the alternatives are actually excellent in their own right. Here's the breakdown:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Soy-free vegan jerky:</strong> Pan's Mushroom Jerky (100% mushroom), Country Archer Plant-Based (mushroom + coconut aminos), Jack & Friends (jackfruit), Off-Piste Provisions (pea + fava protein), AGRO Power Jerky (some varieties). All use coconut aminos or tamari-free bases in place of soy sauce.</li>
    <li><strong>Gluten-free vegan jerky:</strong> All of the above soy-free brands are also gluten-free. Louisville Vegan Jerky and most soy-based brands use regular soy sauce (which contains wheat), so double-check. Some brands offer gluten-free variants — read the label.</li>
    <li><strong>Both soy-free and gluten-free:</strong> Pan's, Country Archer Plant-Based, Jack & Friends, Off-Piste Provisions.</li>
    <li><strong>Free of all top-9 allergens:</strong> Jack & Friends (jackfruit base) is the standout — free of wheat, soy, dairy, egg, tree nuts, peanuts, fish, shellfish, and sesame.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For homemade: substitute coconut aminos for soy sauce in the recipe above to make a fully soy-free, gluten-free version using pea protein or mushroom instead of soy curls.</p>

  <h2 id="why-so-expensive">Why Is Vegan Jerky So Expensive</h2>
  <p><strong>Why is vegan jerky so expensive?</strong> A 2 oz bag routinely runs $6-10, which is on par with (or more than) premium beef jerky per ounce. Three real reasons:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Small-batch manufacturing.</strong> Most vegan jerky brands are still under $50M in annual revenue, which means shorter production runs, higher per-unit costs, and no economies of scale that big meat processors enjoy.</li>
    <li><strong>Ingredient inputs cost more than commodity beef.</strong> Isolated pea protein, mushroom powder, and specialty gluten cost more per pound than the beef trim that goes into commodity jerky. Pan's shiitake mushrooms and Off-Piste's fava protein are premium inputs.</li>
    <li><strong>R&D and texture engineering.</strong> Getting a soy or mushroom base to chew like jerky takes serious food-science work. That development cost is baked into the retail price.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Homemade is the way around this. My soy-curl recipe above runs about <strong>$1.50 per finished ounce</strong> using a $6 bag of Butler soy curls and pantry-standard spices — versus $3-4 per ounce retail. If you eat vegan jerky more than twice a month, learning to make it is the highest-value hour you can spend in the kitchen.</p>

  <h2 id="where-to-buy-vegan-jerky">Where to Buy Vegan Jerky</h2>
  <p>The vegan-jerky category has gone mainstream enough that you don't need a specialty trip anymore. Here's <strong>where to buy vegan jerky</strong> in order of selection:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Whole Foods:</strong> The gold standard. Full lineup of LVJCO, Pan's, Country Archer Plant-Based, and usually 2-3 more. Often in the meat-snack aisle right next to beef jerky, not in the vegan section.</li>
    <li><strong>Sprouts Farmers Market:</strong> Deep vegan jerky assortment, especially LVJCO and Pan's; often has flavors the local Whole Foods doesn't carry.</li>
    <li><strong>Trader Joe's:</strong> Rotating vegan-jerky selection — usually one or two options at a time under the Trader Joe's brand or a rotating third-party brand. Prices tend to be $1-2 lower.</li>
    <li><strong>Amazon:</strong> Every major brand ships nationwide; subscribe-and-save works for LVJCO, Pan's, and Primal Strips. Bulk multipacks are the best price per ounce.</li>
    <li><strong>Direct-from-brand websites:</strong> louisvillejerky.com, pansmushroomjerky.com, primalspiritfoods.com. Best flavor variety and often bundles that beat Amazon on price.</li>
    <li><strong>Conventional grocery (Kroger, Safeway, Publix):</strong> Growing selection — usually Perky Jerky Vegan or Country Archer Plant-Based in the meat-snack aisle. Not deep, but at least one option is usually there.</li>
    <li><strong>Convenience stores:</strong> Almost never stocked, except in coastal-city 7-Elevens carrying LVJCO. Stock up in advance for road trips.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="vegan-jerky-vs-beef-jerky">Vegan Jerky vs Beef Jerky</h2>
  <p>Direct comparison, honestly rendered. This is not a "which is better" question — the right answer depends on why you're eating jerky in the first place. Here is the head-to-head:</p>
  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Factor</th><th>Vegan jerky</th><th>Beef jerky</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Protein per serving</td><td>10–14 g (soy) · 5–7 g (mushroom)</td><td>9–12 g</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fat / saturated fat</td><td>Lower, mostly plant fat</td><td>Higher, mostly saturated</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cholesterol</td><td>0 mg</td><td>25–40 mg</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Sodium</td><td>Similarly high — 300–600 mg</td><td>Similarly high — 400–700 mg</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cost per ounce (retail)</td><td>$3–4</td><td>$2–3 (mid-tier), $4–5 (premium)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cost per ounce (homemade)</td><td>$1.50 (soy curls)</td><td>$1.75–2.50 (90/10 ground beef)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Texture fidelity to "real jerky"</td><td>Very close (soy-based); different but great (mushroom)</td><td>The reference</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Shelf life (sealed)</td><td>6–12 months</td><td>1 year (cured)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Environmental footprint</td><td>Substantially lower</td><td>Substantially higher</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fits which diet</td><td>Vegan, vegetarian, some paleo variants</td><td>Omnivore, paleo, keto</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>For the beef-jerky loyalists reading this: try one bag of Louisville Vegan Jerky Smoked Black Pepper before you form an opinion. It's not going to replace <a href="/how-to-make-beef-jerky/">homemade beef jerky</a> in my rotation — I make jerky for a living, and the real thing is the real thing — but it holds its own as its own snack, and my niece keeps a bag in her car. That counts for something.</p>

  <h2 id="homemade-vs-store-bought">Homemade vs Store-Bought Vegan Jerky</h2>
  <p>If you got this far you already know the answer: try a couple of brands to find a flavor profile you love, then learn to make it at home so you can eat it more often without going broke. Store-bought is for convenience and flavor discovery; homemade is for economics and having a bag in the fridge whenever you want one.</p>
  <p>The recipe up top takes 20 minutes of active work and about an hour in the oven. Batch it on a Sunday, and you have vegan jerky for the whole week at a third of the retail price. If you want more ways to eat plant-based jerky, our <a href="/fruit-jerky/">fruit jerky guide</a> covers the other big non-meat category — dried fruit jerky like mango, pineapple, and apple that hits a completely different flavor profile.</p>

